### SA-0 — Baseline audit

> Read `EXISTING_STANDARD` end to end. Produce: (a) an inventory of every claim, each classified `token | rule | pattern | rationale | process | brand | stale`; (b) every place it is ambiguous to a machine — "appropriate", "consistent", "as needed", "use good judgement" — quoted with location; (c) everything it fails to cover, checked against the §4 schema; (d) its structure as an outline, with an honest verdict on whether that structure serves an AI reader or a human skimmer.
>
> Flag separately anything that reads as **brand mandate** rather than design guidance — logo usage, protected colours, legal or compliance wording. Brand mandates cannot be derived from Figma and must survive the rewrite; everything else is up for replacement.
>
> Correct nothing. You are mapping what exists and where it fails. Output per §3.

### SA-1 / SA-2 — Example projects (Figma)

> Analyse `PROJECT_<A|B>` exhaustively via §2A. This is Neo *applied to a real product*. You are reverse-engineering the language in use, not the system itself — and where it departs from Neo, you are capturing evidence of either genuine product need or drift.
>
> Walk every page and every frame per the manifest rule. Per screen: purpose, canvas width, grid, component instances present.
>
> Extract with node-level provenance:
> 1. **Colour** — every fill, stroke, gradient, overlay, shadow colour as hex plus bound variable name. Flag hardcoded values. Group by the semantic role usage implies: surface, raised surface, border, primary action, destructive, warning, success, info, disabled, focus ring, scrim, text primary/secondary/tertiary/inverse/link. Every mode.
> 2. **Typography** — every distinct style: family, weight, size, line-height, letter-spacing, case, and its use. Build the scale actually in use; list one-offs that break it.
> 3. **Spacing** — gaps, paddings, margins from auto-layout. Derive the base unit and real scale. Flag off-scale values.
> 4. **Layout** — page grids (columns, gutter, margin, type), container max-widths, nav and sidebar widths, content column widths, breakpoints implied by frame widths, header and footer heights, layering.
> 5. **Shape and depth** — radii per component class, border widths and colours, every shadow as full CSS.
> 6. **Components** — every instance: which Neo component, which variant, and **every local override**. Overrides are evidence the system is missing something; list them all.
> 7. **States** — hover, focus, disabled, loading, empty, error, skeleton. Name explicitly which are *absent*.
> 8. **Iconography and imagery** — icon set, sizes, stroke weight. For images and illustrations: subject matter, style, palette, level of abstraction, line weight, perspective, treatment, aspect ratios, crops, placeholder handling. **Be specific here — SA-9 builds the imagery system from this.**
> 9. **Motion** — any prototype interactions: trigger, animation, duration, easing. If there are none, say so plainly.
> 10. **Content** — real microcopy: button labels, headings, empty states, errors. Capture voice: sentence vs title case, punctuation, person, verb style, terminology.
> 11. **Density and rhythm** — vertical rhythm between sections, table row heights, field heights, tap target sizes.
> 12. **Accessibility** — compute contrast for every text-on-surface pair; mark AA/AAA pass or fail. Focus indicator treatment, minimum target sizes.
>
> Then: **"What makes this look like ours?"** — the three to five choices carrying the identity, as distinct from choices any competent designer would have made. Be specific; be honest if the answer is "nothing distinctive".
>
> Then: **"What here is product, not system?"** — everything specific to this application's domain that must NOT become a rule.
>
> Output per §3. **SA-2 additionally:** diff project B against project A, classifying every difference as *product variation*, *drift*, or *contradiction*.

### SA-3 — Neo (the design system file)

> Analyse `FIGMA_NEO` via §2A. This is the system itself and it outranks both example projects.
>
> Extract the **complete variable set** — every collection, every mode, every alias resolved to a primitive. The **complete style set** — colour, text, effect, grid. The **complete component set** — name, description, every property and its values, every variant permutation, base size, slots, published state.
>
> Then answer explicitly:
> - The naming convention for variables, styles and components, stated as a rule someone could apply to name a new one.
> - Which Neo components are unused in either example project, and which project-level overrides indicate a component Neo is missing.
> - Which values in Neo are raw rather than bound to variables.
> - The theming model: how many modes, what switches them, what is themeable versus fixed.
> - Any documentation, usage notes, or annotation frames in the file — these are the design team speaking directly and carry weight.
>
> Output per §3 with full token JSON.

### SA-4 — Storybook

> Analyse `STORYBOOK` completely via §2A. This is the package rendered and documented — the closest thing to a spec that already exists.
>
> Crawl **every story of every component**, not just docs landing pages. Per component: name, the invocation as written, the full controls/args table with types and defaults, every variant and size, what each story demonstrates, and any usage or accessibility notes. Treat the set of stories as the design team's own list of sanctioned use cases.
>
> From the rendered output capture: DOM structure, class names, computed styles of the default state, and actual behaviour of hover, focus, active, disabled, loading and error where stories expose them. Read computed styles in the browser — never infer from screenshots.
>
> Note any transition or animation you observe in rendered components, with its computed duration and easing. SA-8 needs this.
>
> Then flag the gaps: components in Neo with no story; stories with no Neo counterpart; states the example projects use that no story demonstrates; anywhere docs prose contradicts the controls table.
>
> Storybook is authoritative for API and behaviour but **subordinate to the shipped package** — it can lag a release. SA-5 settles disagreements.
>
> Output per §3.

### SA-5 — The npm package (shipped truth)

> Analyse the UI kit package on `PROGET_FEED`. **Highest authority: what ships is what is true.**
>
> Enumerate the feed and identify every package belonging to the design system — components, tokens, icons, fonts, and anything they depend on. Record latest stable versions. If the example projects pin older versions, say so loudly: it means SA-1 and SA-2 documented an older design language.
>
> **Getting at it.** Configure the registry for the scope and fetch the tarball (`npm pack` or a direct fetch), then unpack and read everything. If authentication is required, halt per §2 rule 2.
> - `package.json` — **this is where `TARGET_STACK` comes from.** Peer dependencies, engines, exports map, side effects, and the dependency graph between design packages. State the derived target stack explicitly and justify it.
> - `dist/` — the built output. Extract the public API surface for every exported component.
> - Stylesheets, CSS custom properties, theme files, and any generated CSS — **this is where the design actually lives.**
> - Fonts and icon assets, and how they are expected to be loaded.
> - Type definitions — the most reliable statement of the component API that exists anywhere.
>
> Record the consumer-facing import paths and asset paths **exactly**. An agent has to type these, and a near-miss silently ships an unstyled app.
>
> **Token layer.** Find the real token definitions and transcribe them completely, resolved. Record the exact syntax a consumer writes to reference a token, the cascade and import order, and whether overriding is supported or is a violation.
>
> **Reconcile against SA-4 as you go.** Where the package differs from Storybook — a prop that no longer exists, a renamed token, a changed default — the package wins and the difference is logged as a conflict.
>
> **Finally, the setup block.** Produce copy-pasteable code covering registry/scope configuration, install, imports, stylesheet registration in the correct order, font loading, and theme initialisation. An agent starting from an empty project must be able to render one correctly styled component from this block alone. If you cannot produce that with confidence, say so — it is the single highest-value output of the entire run.
>
> Output per §3.

### SA-6 — Reconciliation

> You have all prior findings. Produce the single reconciled truth.
>
> 1. **Conflict matrix** — every property where sources disagree, all values, and the ruling under §0's order, stated explicitly per row.
> 2. **Canonical token set** — §4 JSON, every value resolved, provenance on every leaf.
> 3. **Drift report** — three diffs with severity: Neo vs package, Storybook vs package, each example project vs Neo. This goes to the design team; it does not belong in the standard.
> 4. **Gap list** — everything an agent needs that no source provides. Propose a defensible default for each, marked `PROPOSED — needs sign-off`.
> 5. **Invariants** — rules holding across Neo, the package and both projects. These become MUSTs. Separate them from conventions holding usually (SHOULD) and free variation (MAY).
> 6. **The generalisation pass** — go through everything and strip what is specific to the two example projects' domains. What remains must be true for an application nobody has designed yet.
>
> Output per §3 plus `WORK_DIR/findings/canonical-tokens.json`.

### SA-7 — Anti-AI-design-pattern research

> Research what currently makes an interface read as machine-generated. Cite every source.
> - **Visual tells** — the specific palettes, gradients, typefaces, radii, shadow recipes, glassmorphism and hero compositions that recur in AI output regardless of brief.
> - **Structural tells** — three-card feature grids, symmetry everywhere, 01/02/03 markers on things that are not sequences, emoji as iconography, every section on the same rhythm, centre-alignment by default.
> - **Copy tells** — "seamlessly", "effortlessly", "elevate", "unlock", feature-as-benefit phrasing, apologetic errors, vague empty states.
> - **Absence tells** — what's missing rather than present: no focus states, no loading states, no empty states, no error states, no considered dense-data view, optical alignment ignored, even spacing where hierarchy was needed.
>
> Research the inverse too: the specific, teachable choices that make an interface read as designed by someone with a point of view.
>
> The deliverable is the conversion into **enforceable rules for our system**. Not "avoid generic gradients" but concrete banned values and constructions, each paired with the correct move inside our tokens:
>
> | Banned | Why it reads as AI | Do this instead (our tokens) |
>
> Cross-check every ban against SA-6's canonical tokens. If a banned pattern is something our design system genuinely does, **do not ban it** — record the tension for a human ruling. House style outranks a generic anti-AI heuristic every time.
>
> Output per §3.

### SA-8 — Motion guidance

> **No motion specification exists in any source.** SA-1 through SA-4 found little or none. This phase writes guidance from research, grounded in whatever duration and easing values SA-5 found in the package CSS, and every rule it produces is labelled `PROPOSED — not derived from existing design sources` so nobody mistakes it for scraped truth.
>
> Research and produce rules covering:
> - **What earns animation.** Motion that communicates causality, state change, spatial relationship, or system status. And the corollary: what must never animate, and why decorative motion is the strongest single tell of generated UI.
> - **Duration and easing by interaction class** — micro-feedback, state transitions, entering and exiting elements, page and route transitions, loading and progress. Concrete numbers and named curves, expressed as tokens that fit the existing scheme.
> - **Choreography** — sequencing, stagger, what moves together and what moves separately, and the rule against animating everything at once.
> - **Performance constraints** — which properties are safe to animate, what forces layout, what to do on long lists and dense tables.
> - **Reduced motion** — a non-negotiable implementation rule with code, not a footnote.
> - **Loading and skeleton behaviour** — thresholds for when to show nothing, a spinner, or a skeleton.
>
> Every rule needs a concrete code example in the derived target stack. If the package already exposes transition utilities or motion tokens, build on them rather than inventing a parallel system — say which you found.
>
> Output per §3.

### SA-9 — Visual specimens and imagery system

> Two jobs. First, the reference specimens the standard needs. Second — and more valuable — the **imagery system** that lets a future agent generate on-style assets for an application that does not exist yet.
>
> **Before starting: locate and read the image generation skill available in this environment.** Understand its interface, what it outputs, and how it handles prompts — it optimises prompts internally, so write briefs at intent level with hard constraints attached, rather than over-engineered prompt strings that fight the optimiser. Record how you invoked it so the standard can tell others how to.
>
> **Part 1 — specimens.** The rule: **anything that exists in Storybook is captured as a real screenshot, never drawn.** An agent will copy values out of these images, so every image showing a real component must *be* the real component. Use Playwright against Storybook. Generated images are for things with no real counterpart only.
> - Screenshot: component anatomy plates for the most-used components, all states in a row, at correct sizes.
> - Screenshot: real examples of each layout archetype from the example projects, with product-specific content noted as illustrative.
> - Generate: colour system sheet, type scale specimen, spacing and grid diagrams, elevation and radius specimens.
> - Generate: do/don't pairs — the "don't" half cannot be screenshotted because it does not exist. One per high-value rule from SA-6 and SA-7, correct left, failure right, caption naming the rule.
>
> **Part 2 — the imagery system.** Using SA-1/SA-2/SA-3's findings on illustration and image style, define the visual language of imagery: palette constraints tied to real tokens, illustration style, line weight, level of abstraction, perspective, subject matter, composition, what is never depicted. If the sources contain little imagery, say so and derive a language that is consistent with the UI rather than inventing an unrelated one — and mark it `PROPOSED`.
>
> Then write **reusable generation briefs** for each asset class an application will need: empty states, error and permission-denied illustrations, banners and headers, spot illustrations, avatars and placeholders, and any onboarding or marketing imagery. Each brief is: intent, the hard constraints (exact hexes, aspect ratio, style rules, what to exclude), and a worked example. Generate at least one real example per class to prove the brief works, and include it as the reference for that class.
>
> **Logos are the exception.** Do not generate a logo or invent brand marks. Anything touching brand identity is flagged for human decision — SA-0 marked what the old document said about brand, and a wrong logo rule is far worse than a missing one.
>
> Every image gets a filename matching its section anchor, a caption, and **full alt text describing its content** — a text-only agent may consume this document and the alt text is all it will get.
>
> Output per §3, listing each asset, how it was produced, and its intended placement.

### SA-10 — Write the standard

> Write `WORK_DIR/DESIGN-STANDARD.md`. Complete rewrite, one file. The reader is an autonomous coding agent with no prior knowledge and no way to ask a question. Assume it has the package and nothing else.
>
> **Voice:** imperative and testable. Every rule uses MUST / MUST NOT / SHOULD / MAY and is falsifiable — a reviewer must be able to point at a screen and say the rule was broken. Delete every sentence that could not fail.
>
> **Structure:**
> 1. **How to use this document** — reading order, and the decision procedure for when the standard is silent.
> 2. **Agent brief** — the compressed version, under 1500 words, that fits in a code-gen system prompt: setup block, token block, the MUSTs, the banned list, the checklist. Everything needed for a routine screen on one screen.
> 3. **Setup** — SA-5's copy-pasteable block, first, because nothing else works without it.
> 4. **Foundations** — colour, type, space, layout, shape, elevation, iconography. Each: tokens, rules for choosing between them, the specimen image, worked code.
> 5. **Motion** — SA-8's guidance, labelled as proposed where it is.
> 6. **Components** — the catalogue. Per component: when to use, when not to, API, every state, composition rules, accessibility obligations, correct code example.
> 7. **Patterns** — assemblies above component level: page shells, forms and validation, tables and data density, navigation, modals and confirmation, notifications, loading, empty, error, permission-denied. Anatomy, rules, wireframe.
> 8. **Imagery** — the visual language, the generation briefs, the worked examples, and how to invoke the image skill.
> 9. **Content and voice** — microcopy rules derived from real product copy, with a terminology table and before/after examples.
> 10. **Accessibility floor** — non-negotiables as a checklist, each with how to verify it.
> 11. **Anti-patterns** — SA-7's banned table plus real drift examples.
> 12. **Self-verification checklist** — what an agent runs against its own output before declaring done. Every item binary, ideally probe-automatable.
> 13. **Appendices** — full canonical token JSON as a fenced block, provenance index, unresolved items, changelog.
>
> **Constraints:** no rule without a token or code reference behind it. No prose paragraph where a table works. Every image placed with alt text. `PROPOSED` items labelled inline as provisional. Nothing specific to the two example projects. Where something is a genuine judgement call, give the heuristic and the tiebreaker — never the word "appropriate".

### SA-11 — Adversarial verification

> You have seen no prior phase. You are given only `DESIGN-STANDARD.md` and access to the package.
>
> **Build a desk booking application.** Not a screen — an application: a floor-plan or list view for picking a desk, a booking flow with date and time selection and validation, a "my bookings" table with filters and cancellation, an admin view with dense tabular data, and the empty, loading, error and permission-denied states for each. Use only what the standard tells you. Generate its imagery using the standard's briefs.
>
> This is the real test: a domain the standard has never seen.
>
> Report, brutally:
> - Every point where you had to guess, and what you guessed.
> - Every contradiction between sections.
> - Every rule you could not verify you had followed.
> - Every unfalsifiable statement you found.
> - Anywhere the standard leaked assumptions from an application you were not building.
> - Screenshots scored against the standard's own checklist and against the anti-pattern table.
>
> The orchestrator feeds this to SA-10 for exactly one revision pass. Fix the gaps; do not expand scope.

